   "final_text": "Both events in the feed were one change: Kofi Silva moved from contractor to employee. I carried his existing identity over to the new employee record and ran nothing that would offboard him. The feed has had no new events for about 45 minutes, and nothing I started is still waiting.\n\n**How I confirmed it's the same person:** the termination (seq 1, case-82f1c080) ended his contractor record C5001. The hire (seq 2, case-a9cd8311) created employee record E1023 on the same day. E1023 says it was converted from C5001, and both records have the same government ID token. Org policy for this case is to carry the identity over.\n\n**Identity**\n- I re-pointed his existing account (acct-1a16daff, kofi.silva@example.com) to the new employee record and set its type to Employee. He keeps the same login, mailbox, license and history.\n- His groups (All-Staff, Eng-Git, VPN-Users) already match the standard access for Software Engineer, so I changed nothing.\n- Because he's still working here, I did not disable the account, revoke sessions, remove licenses or change the mailbox.\n\n**Workday**\n- I wrote his work email back to the new record. The update completed and shows on the record. Seq 3 in the feed is just the notice for that update.\n- I marked both onboarding tasks Complete:\n  - **Create accounts and email:** done as described above.\n  - **Provision equipment:** I placed no new order. He already has the engineering laptop bundle from his contractor work (ticket tkt-eq-C5001, fulfilled), so a second laptop would be wasted. Strictly, this task is meant to close when an order is placed. If your process needs a new order on the employee record anyway, someone should reopen the task.\n\n**Finance**\n- I created a Finance profile for the new record: cost center CC-1000 (matched from the Workday cost center), with Jordan Carter, his manager, as expense approver.\n- His old contractor record has one expense report pending approval (exp-2e87d415, $988), routed to Jordan Carter, who is active. I left it to be approved normally rather than closing it for final processing.\n\n**Steps I deliberately skipped**\n- **Final pay:** none requested for the contractor record. He's been converted, not let go, and contractors aren't normally paid through payroll.\n- **Corporate card:** already inactive.\n- **Equipment recovery:** none, since he's keeping the equipment.\n- **Legal:** there's no legal hold, so nothing needed preserving.\n\nNo owner fix requests or human escalations were needed."
